  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1217 PM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nThe key takeaway of the long term forecast is an unsettled week,\nwith frequent precip chances, increased cloud cover, and cooler \ntemps. For Tues/Wed, the upper pattern will be characterized by a \nweaker upper ridge axis located to our east, extending from E TX \nnorthward onto the east-central Great Plains, and an upper low \nlifting from NV to the northern Plains. Additional lift will be \nprovided by a disturbance embedded within the SSW flow regime \nacross NM. \n\nLocally, this translates to several days of continued moist,\neasterly upslope flow. Forecast soundings again show lower cloud \nbases compared to recent weeks, with uninspiring lapse rates aloft\nkeeping instability values in check, thus the severe potential \nwith any storms on Tues-Wed appears quite limited. While there \nremains some uncertainty on the exact placement of storms Tues- \nWed, the general theme is that precip chances and forecast \nrainfall amounts increase with westward extent (and are highest \nacross eastern New Mexico). For example, the NBM probability of \nexceeding 2\" of rain between Tues AM and Thurs AM is greater than \n50% along the TX/NM border, but decreases sharply to just 15-20% \nalong the I-27 corridor, and less than 10% off the Caprock. This \nexpectation is captured in the Excessive Rainfall Outlooks, which \nkeep the Slight Risk (level 2 out of 4) of flash flooding confined\nto our western FA on Tues-Wed. \n\nThe nocturnal onset of the low-level inversion should trap\nmoisture and keep cloud cover prevalent Tues/Wed night, and I do \nwonder if this will lead to low temps staying a bit warmer than \nthe NBM suggests. At any rate, lows in the 60s and perhaps upper \n50s will be much closer to seasonable values than the conditions \nthat have been in place over the last several weeks. On the flip \nside, all the clouds cover and potential precip should keep highs \nmuch cooler, with much of the Caprock forecast to see highs in the\nupper 70s, roughly 15 degrees cooler than what is forecast for \ntoday. The mid- week disturbance starts to lift north late in the \nweek, but guidance suggests there will be enough forcing to keep \nprecip chances in place locally for Thurs-Fri. As we head into \nnext weekend, the precip chances do trend lower (and temperatures\ntrend warmer) as a subdued ridge axis shifts back west over the \nFA.",
